  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Monday)\nIssued at 123 PM CDT TUE AUG 11 2026\n\nThis afternoon:\nElongated ridging extends from over the Southern \nPlains southeast to across much of the Florida Peninsula while two\nshortwaves are moving southeast over the Ohio River Valley and \nCentral Appalachian regions. Surface high pressure is centered \nacross the far southeast portion of the state with some weakness \nin the ridging across the northern and eastern counties, where \nshower and thunderstorm activity has already developed and is \ncurrently increasing as of early afternoon. Expect chances for \nshowers and storms to be best across the northeast and eastern- \ncentral counties with lowest chances southwest. High temperatures \nwill range from the upper 80s far east to the mid 90s far \nsouthwest. Winds will be from the west to southwest from 4-8 mph.\n\nTonight:\nThe mid-level ridging will consolidate more over the Northern \nGulf Coast region tonight while surface high pressure remains \ncentered just to our southeast. Expect lingering shower and storm \nactivity to decrease through the evening with fair skies overnight\nwith light southwest winds 2-4 mph. Low temperatures will range \nfrom the lower 70s far east and northeast to the mid 70s west and \nsouthwest.\n\nThursday:\nMid-level ridging will remain centered over the Northern\nGulf Coast on Thursday while surface high pressure builds more \nover the southern and western portions of the area, resulting in \nisolated shower and storm activity expected across the eastern \nthird of the area with lowest chances west. High humidity will \npersist and with higher temperatures, will result in heat index \nvalues reaching 105 degrees in portions of our western and central\ncounties. A Heat Advisory will be in effect from 1 pm through 9 \npm.\n\nLong Term:\nThe long-term portion of the forecast will feature \nstrengthening mid-level ridging as it becomes centered over \nCentral Mississippi by this weekend. Surface high pressure will \nmigrate further northwest, becoming centered across the Lower \nMississippi River Valley Region by early in the weekend. This \npattern will result in minimal chances for widely isolated shower \nand storm activity primarily confined to the far east and \nsoutheast portions of the forecast area. Concerns for persistent \nheat stress will increase with a multi-day episode of heat indices\nexceeding 105 degrees and potentially approaching 110 degrees in \nsome spots though the weekend and into early next week. Some spots\nmay fall back into D0 drought category if these conditions do \npersist into middle next week.\n\n05",
